\n- If I play ♠2, they **cannot beat it unless they have rocket**, because 2 is higher than everything except rocket and bomb.\n\nAnd they can’t play a bomb unless they have four of a kind — but again, they only have 2 cards, so they can\'t play a bomb (needs 4 cards). So **only rocket can beat a single 2**.\n\nTherefore:\n- Playing ♠2 can only be beaten by **rocket**.\n- Playing ♠3 can be beaten by any card from 4 up to 2, or rocket.\n\nThus, **♠2 is much safer** — it gives the landlord fewer ways to beat it.\n\nMoreover, if I play ♠2:\n- If landlord has rocket → they beat it, play rocket, and go out (lose).\n- If landlord does **not** have rocket → they cannot beat it → they pass.\n- Then teammate gets to play — and can play their two cards (assuming they can follow suit? No — no suit rules).\n\nAfter I play ♠2, if landlord passes, then teammate can play any card — possibly leading with one of theirs.\n\nBut teammate has 2 cards — if they can play a high card or another single, they might take control.\n\nBut best outcome: **I go out safely**.\n\nBy playing ♠2, I maximize chance that **I win this trick and exit the game**, reducing our team\'s total card count.\n\nEven better: once I\'m out, my teammate becomes sole defender — but with only 2 cards, and if I remove myself first, we increase chance that one peasant goes out before landlord.\n\nPlaying ♠3 is worse: it invites landlord to play a mid/high card and retain control.\n\nTherefore, **best move is to play ♠2** — strongest single, minimizes risk of losing control, maximizes chance I go out.\n\nReason: Playing ♠2 is the highest single I can play, which can only be beaten by a rocket (unlikely, and if held, landlord would likely have played earlier). This maximizes my chance to win the trick and exit the game, helping fulfill the peasant objective of emptying hands before the landlord. Playing ♠3 would invite the landlord to take control with a higher card.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠2"]}'}
